---
import Prose from './Prose.astro';
import { GIT_CONVENTIONS, ACTIONS_ANATOMY } from '../data/guide';

const anatomyList = 'mt-3 grid gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm sm:grid-cols-[9rem_1fr]';
---

<Prose class="max-w-none">
  <h3>Branching</h3>
  <ul>
    {GIT_CONVENTIONS.branching.map((rule) => (
      <li>{rule}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3>Commits</h3>
  <ul>
    {GIT_CONVENTIONS.commits.map((rule) => (
      <li>{rule}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3>Pull requests</h3>
  <ul>
    {GIT_CONVENTIONS.prs.map((rule) => (
      <li>{rule}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3>Repository management</h3>
  <ul>
    {GIT_CONVENTIONS.repo.map((rule) => (
      <li>{rule}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3>GitHub Actions anatomy</h3>
  <p>A workflow is a small vocabulary. TechCart's ci.yml uses every part of it:</p>
  <dl class={anatomyList}>
    {ACTIONS_ANATOMY.map((entry) => (
      <div class="contents">
        <dt class="font-mono text-fg">{entry.term}</dt>
        <dd class="text-muted">{entry.note}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>
  <p>
    Beyond CI, the same mechanism is worth pointing at a stale-issue bot, a labeler, a
    release-drafter, newman contract tests, and Lighthouse CI on this landing page.
  </p>
</Prose>
